0.5 only.\n    50|- §10.5 tool discipline (incl. non-ASCII fallback) — applies whenever a skill writes or edits files, NOT only to section-shape skills. The destructive `edit` UTF-8 bug observed 2026-05-19 zeroed out a target file on encoding failure; the §10.5.1 fallback (use full-file replace instead of edit when non-ASCII content is present) is the workaround until the runtime tool is fixed.\n    51|- §11 harness output sidecar — last write before `final_response`. Without it the orchestrator records `outputs: {}` and downstream steps cannot pick up declared output parameters. **Exception:** sandbox-mode skills skip §11 because Stepwise owns the apply-back step; see the `refining-artifact-consistency` and `conducting-excursion` special routes above (`§10.5 only`).\n    52|- §11.6 open questions in the sidecar — whenever you wrote an `## Open Questions` section into an artifact, mirror it into the reserved `_open_questions` key of that same sidecar. Zero-Invention already tells you to raise an open question rather than guess (§9); this is what makes the raised question trackable instead of prose the orchestrator has to guess at. Same exception as §11.\n    53|- §4 Memory Bank session-end writes (`active-context.md`, `progress.md`) — only when the skill's SKILL.md explicitly opts in; otherwise skip.\n    54|- §7 REPAIR bookkeeping — when running in REPAIR mode (a `## ⚠️ Repair feedback` block is present), editing the artifact in place is NOT enough: you MUST also **bump the spec `version`** (semver patch, e.g. `1.0.0` → `1.0.1`) AND **record what changed in the AUDIT file** (set `mode: REPAIR` and append a `## Repair History` entry). Skipping this leaves the version and audit trail stale even though the content was fixed — the documented \"iteration applied but version/audit not updated\" defect. See §7.2 steps 7–8 for the exact fields and the mandatory self-check.\n    55|\n    56|**REPAIR mode override:** if the prompt contains a `## ⚠️ Repair feedback` block, read §7 (REPAIR Mode) before any other section — even when §7 is not listed in your route. §7 carries the version-bump + audit-delta bookkeeping that is mandatory for every REPAIR (see the universal rule above); the routing rule above intentionally omits it to stay short, so this override is the authoritative trigger.\n    57|\n    58|**REBUILD mode override (discard):** if the prompt contains a `## ♻️ Rebuild from scratch` block instead of `## ⚠️ Repair feedback`, read §7.3 (REBUILD Mode) before any other section. This is a DISCARD, not an iterate: the reviewer judged the artifact fundamentally wrong, so you must REGENERATE it from scratch (full BUILD) rather than surgically repair it — while still overwriting the SAME file/SESSION_ID (never duplicate) and doing the version-bump + audit bookkeeping (`mode: REBUILD`). REBUILD and REPAIR are mutually exclusive: only one of the two blocks is ever present.\n    59|\n    60|## Section Index\n    61|\n    62|- §1  — Session Identity (SESSION_ID format, REPAIR reuse rules)\n    63|- §2  — `_progress.json` early-signal write (FIRST ACTION / LAST ACTION)\n    64|  - §2.a Seed `total` once the item count is known (never leave `total: 0`)\n    65|  - §2.b Co-locate the tracker update with the checkpoint write in every production loop\n    66|  - §2.1 Pre-completion self-validation gate (universal — completeness · placeholders · grounding · format)\n    67|- §3  — IMPL-STATE schema (implementing-code only)\n    68|- §4  — Memory Bank cross-session continuity (`active-context.md`, `progress.md`)\n    69|- §5  — FIC context monitoring and recovery checkpoint\n    70|- §6  — Dual-format input detection\n    71|- §7  — REPAIR mode (when `failure_feedback` is provided); §7.3 — REBUILD mode (discard → regenerate)\n    72|- §8  — Deviation taxonomy (Auto-Fix / Escalate / Defer)\n    73|- §9  — Non-interactive execution rules\n    74|- §10 — Section-by-section spec generation (skeleton-first + one-section-per-write)\n    75|  - §10.1 Section-shape `_progress.json` schema\n    76|  - §10.2 Source extraction pass\n    77|  - §10.3 Phase A — Skeleton-first\n    78|  - §10.4 Phase B — Populate, one section at a time\n    79|  - §10.5 Tool discipline (hard rules, includes non-ASCII fallback)\n    80|    - §10.5.3 Tool-output retention (context-budget: run full, retain only the verdict / failing slice / distilled evidence)\n    81|  - §10.6 CONTINUE on re-entry\n    82|- §11 — Harness output sidecar (required when running under Stepwise)\n    83|  - §11.1.1 Path correctness (report the path you actually wrote to)\n    84|  - §11.1.2 Verdict fidelity (closed-set tokens verbatim; synonyms fail closed)\n    85|  - §11.6 Open Questions in the sidecar (mirror any `## Open Questions` section you wrote)\n    86|- §12 — Delegated exploration (read-only subagent fan-out, harness-agnostic, optional)\n    87|  - §12.1 When to delegate (and when not)\n    88|  - §12.2 The return contract (conclusions + `file:line`, never file dumps)\n    89|    - §12.2.1 Anti-patterns — hard stops (no verbatim dumps, no known paths, no duplicate reads)\n    90|  - §12.3 Model selection (prefer the cheap/fast tier)\n    91|  - §12.4 Verify before use (subagent output is input, not ground truth)\n    92|- §13 — Code-location discipline (consult `codebase-map.md` before any discovery scan; code-searching skills only)\n    93|  - §13.1 The rule — consult before scan, not never scan\n    94|  - §13.2 When the map is insufficient — flag the gap\n    95|  - §13.3 Applies to (code-searching skills only)\n    96|  - §13.4 Composition with §12\n    97|  - §12.5 Fallback (no subagent capability → inline exploration)\n    98|  - §12.6 Skill-level enforcement\n    99|- §14 — Parallel fan-out (write-capable subagent fan-out over independent units; harness-agnostic, optional)\n   100|  - §14.1 When to fan out (and when not)\n</file>","isError":false}}
